<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Consider the curve <m>y = {6 x-6 x^2}</m> for <m>0 \le x \le {3}</m>.</p><p>(a) Find the <m>x</m>-coordinate, other than <m>0</m>, where the curve crosses the <m>x</m>-axis.</p><div><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Find the exact value of <m>\displaystyle \int_0^{ {3} } y\,dx</m>.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/>  </div><p>(c) Find the exact total area between the curve and the <m>x</m>-axis from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = {3}</m>.</p><div>Area <m>=</m> <fillin characters="8" name="ans3"/> square units  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Sketch first. The curve crosses the <m>x</m>-axis inside the interval, so part of the region is above the axis and part below. The definite integral counts the part below as negative; the area adds the sizes of the parts.</p><p>(a) <m>y = {-6 \left(x-1\right) x}</m>, so <m>y = 0</m> when <m>x = 0</m> or <m>x = {1}</m>.</p><p>(b) <me>\int_0^{ {3} } \left({6 x-6 x^2}\right)dx = \left[{3 x^2-2 x^3}\right]_0^{ {3} } = {-27}</me></p><p>(c) <m>\displaystyle \int_0^{ {1} } y\,dx = {1}</m> and <m>\displaystyle \int_{ {1} }^{ {3} } y\,dx = {-28}</m>. Adding the sizes, the total area is <m>{1} + {28} = {29}</m> square units.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>1</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>-27</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>29</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>